{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793479920,"nonce":"cvVTtkKdRTQNWAlcEGEg6FPOQAE=","title":"Lua: official files.lua fails at line 762 on Linux only when cargo test runs it","message":"On the NUC (x86_64 Linux), official_complete/files.lua stops with \"files.lua:762: assertion failed!\" right after \"(some error messages are expected now)\" whenever `cargo test --release -p zyntax_lua --features llvm-backend --test conformance -- official_complete` runs it: three runs out of three, with this session's binary and with dd108e8b's put in place of cargo's artifact. The same binary passes the whole file run directly, from Python with the harness's descriptor limit and captured pipes, two copies concurrently in the same directory, and the conformance test binary run by hand outside cargo (18 pass, 0 regressions). Line 762 is the exit-code check of the popen/os.execute table (`exit 3`, `exit 129`, the HUP and KILL signals, `zylua -e \"os.exit(20, true)\"`), so under cargo one of those commands returns a different nonzero status; something cargo's environment adds (LD_LIBRARY_PATH with the LLVM directories, inherited jobserver descriptors) reaches the child processes. The Mac passes the same test under cargo.","files":null}]}